Task 1: Understanding and Configuring HBAC Rules
Subtask 1.1: Explore Current HBAC Configuration
First, let's examine the existing HBAC (Host-Based Access Control) rules in your IdM environment.

Connect to the IdM server using the provided credentials:
# SSH to the IdM server
ssh admin@idm-server.example.com
Authenticate with Kerberos:
# Obtain Kerberos ticket
kinit admin
# Enter the admin password when prompted
List existing HBAC rules:
# View all current HBAC rules
ipa hbacrule-find

# Get detailed information about the default rule
ipa hbacrule-show allow_all
Examine HBAC rule components:
# List available users for HBAC rules
ipa user-find --all

# List available groups
ipa group-find --all

# List enrolled hosts
ipa host-find --all

# List available services
ipa hbacsvc-find
Subtask 1.2: Create Custom HBAC Rules
Now we'll create specific access control rules to restrict access based on user groups.

Disable the default allow_all rule (this allows everyone access to everything):
# Disable the permissive default rule
ipa hbacrule-disable allow_all

# Verify the rule is disabled
ipa hbacrule-show allow_all
Create a rule for administrators:
# Create HBAC rule for admin access
ipa hbacrule-add admin_access \
  --desc="Allow administrators full access to all systems"

# Add the admins group to the rule
ipa hbacrule-add-user admin_access --groups=admins

# Allow access to all hosts
ipa hbacrule-add-host admin_access --hosts=idm-server.example.com
ipa hbacrule-add-host admin_access --hosts=client1.example.com
ipa hbacrule-add-host admin_access --hosts=client2.example.com

# Allow all services
ipa hbacrule-add-service admin_access --hbacsvcs=sshd
ipa hbacrule-add-service admin_access --hbacsvcs=login
ipa hbacrule-add-service admin_access --hbacsvcs=su
ipa hbacrule-add-service admin_access --hbacsvcs=sudo
Create a rule for regular users:
# Create HBAC rule for regular user access
ipa hbacrule-add user_workstation_access \
  --desc="Allow regular users access to workstation services only"

# Add the users group to the rule
ipa hbacrule-add-user user_workstation_access --groups=users

# Allow access to client machines only (not the server)
ipa hbacrule-add-host user_workstation_access --hosts=client1.example.com
ipa hbacrule-add-host user_workstation_access --hosts=client2.example.com

# Allow only SSH and login services
ipa hbacrule-add-service user_workstation_access --hbacsvcs=sshd
ipa hbacrule-add-service user_workstation_access --hbacsvcs=login
Create a rule for developers:
# Create HBAC rule for developer access
ipa hbacrule-add developer_access \
  --desc="Allow developers access to development systems with sudo"

# Add the developers group to the rule
ipa hbacrule-add-user developer_access --groups=developers

# Allow access to specific development hosts
ipa hbacrule-add-host developer_access --hosts=client1.example.com

# Allow SSH, login, and sudo services
ipa hbacrule-add-service developer_access --hbacsvcs=sshd
ipa hbacrule-add-service developer_access --hbacsvcs=login
ipa hbacrule-add-service developer_access --hbacsvcs=sudo
Subtask 1.3: Verify HBAC Rule Configuration
Display all HBAC rules:
# List all HBAC rules with their status
ipa hbacrule-find --all

# Show detailed configuration of each rule
ipa hbacrule-show admin_access --all
ipa hbacrule-show user_workstation_access --all
ipa hbacrule-show developer_access --all
Test HBAC rule syntax:
# Test if a specific user can access a host/service combination
ipa hbactest --user=testuser1 --host=client1.example.com --service=sshd
ipa hbactest --user=admin --host=idm-server.example.com --service=sshd
ipa hbactest --user=developer1 --host=client2.example.com --service=sudo
Task 2: Applying Group-Based Access Policies
Subtask 2.1: Create Test Users and Groups
Create user groups if they don't already exist:
# Create developers group
ipa group-add developers --desc="Development team members"

# Create users group (may already exist)
ipa group-add users --desc="Regular system users" || echo "Group already exists"

# Verify admins group exists
ipa group-show admins
Create test users:
# Create test users for different groups
ipa user-add testuser1 \
  --first=Test --last=User1 \
  --password

# Create developer user
ipa user-add developer1 \
  --first=Developer --last=One \
  --password

# Create admin user
ipa user-add testadmin \
  --first=Test --last=Admin \
  --password
Add users to appropriate groups:
# Add testuser1 to users group
ipa group-add-member users --users=testuser1

# Add developer1 to developers group
ipa group-add-member developers --users=developer1

# Add testadmin to admins group
ipa group-add-member admins --users=testadmin

# Verify group memberships
